  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and muggy throughout Friday. Heat Advisory \ncriteria is expected to be met in the Bangor/Penobscot Region, \nsouthern Central Highlands, and portions of Downeast Maine. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHot and muggy temperatures expected again tomorrow with highs \nclimbing near to possibly above 90. Dewpoints will also climb to\naround 70 south and east of State Route 6. This will produce \nheat indices or feels like temps in the mid 90s for several \nhours tomorrow afternoon. This will mostly likely affect lower \nelevations in southern Piscataquis and central Penobscot \ncounties, as well as the Bangor Region over toward Calais and \ndown into MDI. The record high temp at Caribou on Friday is in \njeopardy with a forecast temp of 88. The record is 89 degrees\nfrom 2022. All other temps are forecast to fall about 3-5 \ndegrees short of their records. \n\nUltimately, the heat indices will depend on how warm it is able to \nget tomorrow. The potential of a storm or two developing on a weak \nsurface trof in that area during the afternoon may limit heating \nthough not all of the guidance is showing development. \n\nOver the weekend, temperatures will be not quite as hot mainly due \nto increased clouds. That said, it will still easily be warmer than \naverage, with nighttime temperatures staying especially mild. And it \nwill still be muggy over the weekend. Uncertainty in temperatures\nover the weekend is just how warm it gets and that will depend \non the amount of clouds and showers/storms. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some severe storms are possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system slowly moves northeast through Canada.\nAssociated cold front slowly moves through Maine from Friday \nnight into Sunday. There is some instability ahead of this cold\nfront, with the best instability and moisture on Saturday. \nModels continue to align with 1,500 2,000 J/kg of CAPE, good low\nlevel lapse rates (roughly 8C/km), and plenty of precipitation.\nSoundings are moist, with classic long and skinny CAPE \nthroughout most of northern and eastern Maine. PWAT values \naround 1.8 2.0, which is well above the Saturday daily record of\n1.7\". Continuing to keep the threat of thunder inland on \nSaturday, as a seabreeze off of the cold Gulf of Maine waters \nshould stabilize and keep daytime high temperatures on the lower\nside along Downeast Maine coastline through the weekend. Storm \nmotions based on Corfidi vectors seem to have trended a bit \nslower, but still not anticipating flash flood concerns. Three \nhour flash flood guidance is roughly 1.5 - 2 in the north, and \n2-3 Downeast. Currently, looking at maybe 0.5-1 of rain, but \nareas with convection could produce higher totals. \n\nCurrently, models are under predicting the strength of \nAtlantic Bermuda High, which could impact the timing and track \nof the impending system. Models seem to agree that the high \nshould weaken by the weekend, but if not, this could impact \nrainfall totals. Will have to monitor as CAMs pick up on low \npressure system. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Scattered rain showers and thunderstorms early\nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nRounds of shortwaves move through the north early next week,\nbringing a chance for rain showers and some thunderstorms.\nThunder possible in the north on Monday, and areawide Tuesday.\nEnvironment does not look too impressive for strong \nthunderstorms, so could just be some brief isolated storms below\nsevere criteria.",
